#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --quiet --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = ["mutagen>=1.47"]
# ///
"""Look up a song on MusicBrainz and write its tags.

    autotag FILE [FILE ...]
    autotag --artist "Guts" --title "Aimer sans amour" FILE.mp3

Files are MP3, AAC or ALAC in .m4a, WAV and AIFF. The track is found by
audio fingerprint (AcoustID) when a key is available, otherwise by
searching "Artist - Title" taken from the file name or the existing tags.
Candidates are listed, then the tag changes are shown and written only
after confirmation. A field MusicBrainz has no value for keeps its current
value. A track MusicBrainz does not know (a mashup, a YouTube remix) can
have its tags typed in instead. Tags are written as ID3v2.3 (MP3, and the
ID3 chunk of WAV and AIFF) or as MP4 atoms, and the junk left by yt-dlp
(YouTube description, URL, MP4 brand) is removed. Artwork is left alone.

Once tagged, the file is moved into the library (--copy keeps the original)
the way iTunes laid it out: Artist/Album/NN Title.mp3, D-NN on albums with
several discs, Compilations/Album for Various Artists, folder names cut at
40 characters and file names at 36. An artist or album folder that already
exists is reused, matched without regard to case or Unicode form; missing
ones are created. A file already at the destination is never replaced, and a
song of the same title already filed under the artist is reported before
anything is copied.

    autotag --export-only FILE        # file it with the tags it has
    autotag --no-export FILE          # tag it, leave it where it is

    autotag serve [--library DIR]     # JSON lines on stdin and stdout, for iprune

AcoustID key (free, https://acoustid.org/new-application): set ACOUSTID_KEY
or put it in ~/.config/autotag/acoustid.key. Fingerprinting needs fpcalc
(pacman -S chromaprint).
"""

